%%meta name: Service Management Reporting
%%meta kind: reports
%%report open-case-backlog title: Open case backlog by priority entity: Case chart: bar x: priority y: open_cases help: Shows managers where unresolved customer demand is concentrated by urgency. sql: SELECT priority, COUNT(*) AS open_cases FROM bus_case WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status NOT IN ('closed','cancelled') GROUP BY priority ORDER BY open_cases DESC
%%report unassigned-cases title: Unassigned active cases entity: Case help: Finds accepted customer work with no accountable agent before it ages or breaches. sql: SELECT case_number, name, priority, status, created_at FROM bus_case WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND owner_id IS NULL AND status NOT IN ('closed','cancelled') ORDER BY created_at
%%report case-aging-by-queue title: Case aging by queue entity: Case chart: bar x: queue_name y: average_age_hours help: Compares active backlog age across queues so supervisors can rebalance capacity. sql: SELECT q.name AS queue_name, AVG(EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (CURRENT_TIMESTAMP - c.created_at))/3600) AS average_age_hours FROM bus_case c JOIN bus_queue q ON q.id = c.queue_id WHERE c.deleted_at IS NULL AND q.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.status NOT IN ('closed','cancelled') GROUP BY q.name ORDER BY average_age_hours DESC
%%report sla-breach-rate title: SLA milestone breach rate entity: CaseMilestone chart: bar x: milestone_type y: breach_rate help: Measures the proportion of completed or elapsed service clocks that breached their contractual target. sql: SELECT milestone_type, 100.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'breached' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) / NULLIF(COUNT(*),0) AS breach_rate FROM bus_case_milestone WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY milestone_type ORDER BY breach_rate DESC
%%report cases-nearing-sla title: Cases approaching SLA deadline entity: CaseMilestone help: Gives agents and managers a forward-looking list of active clocks due within two hours. sql: SELECT c.case_number, m.milestone_type, m.target_at, c.priority, c.status FROM bus_case_milestone m JOIN bus_case c ON c.id = m.case_id WHERE m.deleted_at IS NULL AND c.deleted_at IS NULL AND m.status = 'active' AND m.target_at <= CURRENT_TIMESTAMP + INTERVAL '2 hours' ORDER BY m.target_at
%%report first-contact-resolution title: First-contact resolution proxy entity: Case chart: line x: month y: resolution_rate help: Tracks resolved cases that did not reopen, grouped by closure month as a service-effectiveness indicator. sql: SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', closed_at) AS month, 100.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'closed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) / NULLIF(COUNT(*),0) AS resolution_rate FROM bus_case WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND closed_at IS NOT NULL GROUP BY month ORDER BY month
%%report channel-demand title: Case demand by origin entity: Case chart: pie x: origin y: case_count help: Shows which customer channels create service demand and informs channel investment and staffing. sql: SELECT origin, COUNT(*) AS case_count FROM bus_case WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY origin ORDER BY case_count DESC
%%report agent-workload title: Active routed work by agent entity: AgentWork chart: bar x: agent_name y: active_items help: Shows current accepted and active omnichannel work so supervisors can identify overload and idle capacity. sql: SELECT u.name AS agent_name, COUNT(*) AS active_items FROM bus_agent_work aw JOIN bus_user u ON u.id = aw.user_id WHERE aw.deleted_at IS NULL AND u.deleted_at IS NULL AND aw.status IN ('accepted','active') GROUP BY u.name ORDER BY active_items DESC
%%report queue-waiting-work title: Waiting routed work by queue entity: AgentWork chart: bar x: queue_name y: waiting_items help: Highlights queues where offered work has not yet been accepted. sql: SELECT q.name AS queue_name, COUNT(*) AS waiting_items FROM bus_agent_work aw JOIN bus_queue q ON q.id = aw.queue_id WHERE aw.deleted_at IS NULL AND q.deleted_at IS NULL AND aw.status IN ('pending','offered') GROUP BY q.name ORDER BY waiting_items DESC
%%report conversation-containment title: Automated conversation containment entity: BotSession chart: bar x: status y: sessions help: Compares bot completion, failure, and human handoff volumes to monitor safe automation effectiveness. sql: SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS sessions FROM bus_bot_session WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY status ORDER BY sessions DESC
%%report knowledge-effectiveness title: Knowledge use and helpfulness entity: CaseArticle chart: bar x: usage_type y: helpful_links help: Shows which knowledge delivery paths contribute to case resolution and which need editorial improvement. sql: SELECT usage_type, SUM(CASE WHEN helpful THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS helpful_links FROM bus_case_article WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY usage_type ORDER BY helpful_links DESC
%%report stale-published-knowledge title: Published knowledge requiring review entity: KnowledgeArticle help: Lists published articles whose governance activity is oldest so knowledge managers can schedule validation or retirement. sql: SELECT article_number, name, article_type, created_at FROM bus_knowledge_article WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status = 'published' ORDER BY created_at
%%report major-incident-status title: Major incident status and impact entity: Incident chart: bar x: status y: incident_count help: Summarises active disruption volume and lifecycle position for command and executive review. sql: SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS incident_count FROM bus_incident WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY status ORDER BY incident_count DESC
%%report high-risk-change-pipeline title: High-risk change pipeline entity: ChangeRequest chart: bar x: status y: change_count help: Shows high and critical changes awaiting assessment, approval, implementation, validation, or rollback. sql: SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS change_count FROM bus_change_request WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND risk_level IN ('high','critical') GROUP BY status ORDER BY change_count DESC
%%report failed-change-rate title: Failed and rolled-back changes entity: ChangeRequest chart: line x: month y: failed_changes help: Tracks unsuccessful production changes over time for reliability and CAB improvement. sql: SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', created_at) AS month, COUNT(*) AS failed_changes FROM bus_change_request WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status IN ('failed','rolled_back') GROUP BY month ORDER BY month
%%report field-appointment-performance title: Field appointment outcomes entity: ServiceAppointment chart: bar x: status y: appointments help: Compares completed, cancelled, and cannot-complete visits to expose scheduling, parts, or skills problems. sql: SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS appointments FROM bus_service_appointment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY status ORDER BY appointments DESC
%%report technician-utilisation title: Scheduled technician hours entity: ResourceAssignment chart: bar x: technician y: scheduled_hours help: Measures reserved appointment time by technician for workload balancing and capacity planning. sql: SELECT sr.name AS technician, SUM(EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (ra.assigned_end - ra.assigned_start))/3600) AS scheduled_hours FROM bus_resource_assignment ra JOIN bus_service_resource sr ON sr.id = ra.service_resource_id WHERE ra.deleted_at IS NULL AND sr.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY sr.name ORDER BY scheduled_hours DESC
%%report parts-below-reorder title: Service parts below reorder point entity: InventoryItem help: Lists stock balances that cannot safely meet expected field demand after current reservations. sql: SELECT l.name AS location, p.name AS product, i.on_hand_quantity, i.reserved_quantity, i.reorder_point FROM bus_inventory_item i JOIN bus_location l ON l.id = i.location_id JOIN bus_product p ON p.id = i.product_id WHERE i.deleted_at IS NULL AND l.deleted_at IS NULL AND p.deleted_at IS NULL AND i.on_hand_quantity - i.reserved_quantity <= i.reorder_point ORDER BY location, product
%%report return-disposition title: Returned product disposition entity: ReturnOrderLine chart: pie x: disposition y: returned_units help: Shows whether returned units are restocked, repaired, scrapped, sent to vendors, or returned to customers. sql: SELECT disposition, SUM(quantity) AS returned_units FROM bus_return_order_line WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY disposition ORDER BY returned_units DESC
%%report forecast-staffing-gap title: Forecast staffing gap entity: Forecast chart: bar x: interval_start y: required_agents help: Shows required agents by planning interval so workforce managers can compare forecast demand with published shifts. sql: SELECT interval_start, SUM(required_agents) AS required_agents FROM bus_forecast WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY interval_start ORDER BY interval_start
%%report quality-score-by-agent title: Quality score by agent entity: QualityReview chart: bar x: agent_name y: average_score help: Compares calibrated interaction quality while preserving the review evidence behind coaching. sql: SELECT u.name AS agent_name, AVG(qr.score) AS average_score FROM bus_quality_review qr JOIN bus_user u ON u.id = qr.user_id WHERE qr.deleted_at IS NULL AND u.deleted_at IS NULL AND qr.status = 'final' GROUP BY u.name ORDER BY average_score DESC
%%report customer-satisfaction title: Customer satisfaction trend entity: SurveyResponse chart: line x: month y: average_score help: Tracks customer ratings over time and provides the top-level service outcome measure. sql: SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', created_at) AS month, AVG(score) AS average_score FROM bus_survey_response WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY month ORDER BY month
%%report negative-feedback-recovery title: Negative feedback requiring recovery entity: SurveyResponse help: Gives service managers the customer comments and cases that need a closed-loop recovery response. sql: SELECT sr.created_at, sr.score, sr.sentiment, sr.comment, c.case_number FROM bus_survey_response sr LEFT JOIN bus_case c ON c.id = sr.case_id WHERE sr.deleted_at IS NULL AND sr.sentiment = 'negative' ORDER BY sr.created_at DESC
%%report integration-dead-letter title: Failed integration events entity: IntegrationEvent help: Lists dead-lettered and repeatedly failing events with correlation keys for operational recovery. sql: SELECT name, event_type, correlation_key, status, attempt_count, created_at FROM bus_integration_event WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status IN ('retrying','dead_lettered') ORDER BY attempt_count DESC, created_at
%%report ai-oversight-outcomes title: AI outcomes and human oversight entity: AiInteraction chart: bar x: outcome y: interactions help: Shows acceptance, edits, rejection, escalation, and policy blocks across governed AI service interactions. sql: SELECT outcome, COUNT(*) AS interactions FROM bus_ai_interaction WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY outcome ORDER BY interactions DESC
%%report ai-low-confidence-review title: Low-confidence AI interactions entity: AiInteraction help: Lists AI outputs below the supervision threshold for governance review and prompt or policy improvement. sql: SELECT name, case_id, user_id, outcome, confidence, rationale, created_at FROM bus_ai_interaction WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND confidence < 0.7 ORDER BY confidence, created_at DESC
%%report consent-exceptions title: Consent exceptions by purpose entity: Consent chart: bar x: purpose y: restricted_contacts help: Counts denied, withdrawn, and expired consent choices that automations and agents must honour. sql: SELECT purpose, COUNT(*) AS restricted_contacts FROM bus_consent WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND status IN ('denied','withdrawn','expired') GROUP BY purpose ORDER BY restricted_contacts DESC
%%report audit-sensitive-actions title: Sensitive actions by type entity: AuditEvent chart: bar x: action_type y: action_count help: Summarises exports, deletes, approvals, logins, and permission changes for security review. sql: SELECT action_type, COUNT(*) AS action_count FROM bus_audit_event WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND action_type IN ('export','delete','approve','reject','login','permission_change') GROUP BY action_type ORDER BY action_count DESC
%%report tenant-by-status title: Tenants by status entity: Tenant chart: pie x: status y: records help: A separately governed service organisation whose data, users, policies, and reporting are isolated from every other tenant. How tenants divide across status, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_tenant WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report tenant-created-per-month title: Tenants created per month entity: Tenant chart: line x: month y: records help: A separately governed service organisation whose data, users, policies, and reporting are isolated from every other tenant. New tenants per month over two years - the volume the business actually handles. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_tenant WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
%%report tenant-top-region title: Tenants: the most common region entity: Tenant chart: bar x: region y: records help: A separately governed service organisation whose data, users, policies, and reporting are isolated from every other tenant. The ten most frequent values of region among tenants; free text that repeats is a list the model could close. sql: SELECT COALESCE(region, '(blank)') AS region, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_tenant WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report business-unit-by-status title: Business Units by status entity: BusinessUnit chart: pie x: status y: records help: An operating division, brand, region, or service centre with its own queues, hours, managers, and performance targets. How business units divide across status, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_business_unit WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report business-unit-created-per-month title: Business Units created per month entity: BusinessUnit chart: line x: month y: records help: An operating division, brand, region, or service centre with its own queues, hours, managers, and performance targets. New business units per month over two years - the volume the business actually handles. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_business_unit WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
%%report business-unit-per-tenant title: Business Units per tenant entity: BusinessUnit chart: bar x: tenant y: records help: An operating division, brand, region, or service centre with its own queues, hours, managers, and performance targets. Which tenant the most business units belong to; the head of the list is the concentration to know about. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS tenant, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_business_unit x LEFT JOIN bus_tenant p ON p.id = x.tenant_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report user-by-status title: Users by status entity: User chart: pie x: status y: records help: A named employee, contractor, partner, or administrator who signs in, owns work, approves decisions, or appears in the audit trail. How users divide across status, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_user WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report user-by-user-type title: Users by user type entity: User chart: pie x: user_type y: records help: A named employee, contractor, partner, or administrator who signs in, owns work, approves decisions, or appears in the audit trail. How users divide across user type, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(user_type::text, '(unset)') AS user_type, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_user WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report user-per-business-unit title: Users per business unit entity: User chart: bar x: business_unit y: records help: A named employee, contractor, partner, or administrator who signs in, owns work, approves decisions, or appears in the audit trail. Which business unit the most users belong to; the head of the list is the concentration to know about. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS business_unit, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_user x LEFT JOIN bus_business_unit p ON p.id = x.business_unit_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report role-assignment-by-role-name title: Role Assignments by role name entity: RoleAssignment chart: bar x: role_name y: records help: A time-bounded grant connecting a user to a governed service role, optionally limited to one operating unit. How role assignments divide across role name, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(role_name::text, '(unset)') AS role_name, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_role_assignment WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report role-assignment-per-user title: Role Assignments per user entity: RoleAssignment chart: bar x: user y: records help: A time-bounded grant connecting a user to a governed service role, optionally limited to one operating unit. Which user the most role assignments belong to; the head of the list is the concentration to know about. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS user, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_role_assignment x LEFT JOIN bus_user p ON p.id = x.user_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report role-assignment-per-business-unit title: Role Assignments per business unit entity: RoleAssignment chart: bar x: business_unit y: records help: A time-bounded grant connecting a user to a governed service role, optionally limited to one operating unit. Which business unit the most role assignments belong to; the head of the list is the concentration to know about. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS business_unit, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_role_assignment x LEFT JOIN bus_business_unit p ON p.id = x.business_unit_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report account-by-status title: Accounts by status entity: Account chart: pie x: status y: records help: A customer organisation or household whose contacts, products, contracts, entitlements, cases, and service history are managed together. How accounts divide across status, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(status::text, '(unset)') AS status, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_account WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report account-by-tier title: Accounts by tier entity: Account chart: pie x: tier y: records help: A customer organisation or household whose contacts, products, contracts, entitlements, cases, and service history are managed together. How accounts divide across tier, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(tier::text, '(unset)') AS tier, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_account WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report account-per-tenant title: Accounts per tenant entity: Account chart: bar x: tenant y: records help: A customer organisation or household whose contacts, products, contracts, entitlements, cases, and service history are managed together. Which tenant the most accounts belong to; the head of the list is the concentration to know about. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS tenant, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_account x LEFT JOIN bus_tenant p ON p.id = x.tenant_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report contact-by-preferred-channel title: Contacts by preferred channel entity: Contact chart: bar x: preferred_channel y: records help: A customer, consumer, or authorised representative who requests help and receives service communications. How contacts divide across preferred channel, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(preferred_channel::text, '(unset)') AS preferred_channel, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_contact WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report contact-created-per-month title: Contacts created per month entity: Contact chart: line x: month y: records help: A customer, consumer, or authorised representative who requests help and receives service communications. New contacts per month over two years - the volume the business actually handles. sql: SELECT to_char(date_trunc('month', created_at), 'YYYY-MM') AS month, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_contact WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND created_at >= now() - interval '24 months' GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1
%%report contact-per-account title: Contacts per account entity: Contact chart: bar x: account y: records help: A customer, consumer, or authorised representative who requests help and receives service communications. Which account the most contacts belong to; the head of the list is the concentration to know about. sql: SELECT COALESCE(p.name::text, '(none)') AS account, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_contact x LEFT JOIN bus_account p ON p.id = x.account_id AND p.deleted_at IS NULL WHERE x.deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report address-by-address-type title: Addresses by address type entity: Address chart: pie x: address_type y: records help: A reusable customer location retained separately so service, shipping, billing, and historic addresses are not overwritten. How addresses divide across address type, so the dominant group and the empty ones are both visible. sql: SELECT COALESCE(address_type::text, '(unset)') AS address_type, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_address WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
%%report address-top-city title: Addresses: the most common city entity: Address chart: bar x: city y: records help: A reusable customer location retained separately so service, shipping, billing, and historic addresses are not overwritten. The ten most frequent values of city among addresses; free text that repeats is a list the model could close. sql: SELECT COALESCE(city, '(blank)') AS city, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_address WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
%%report address-top-country-code title: Addresses: the most common country code entity: Address chart: bar x: country_code y: records help: A reusable customer location retained separately so service, shipping, billing, and historic addresses are not overwritten. The ten most frequent values of country code among addresses; free text that repeats is a list the model could close. sql: SELECT COALESCE(country_code, '(blank)') AS country_code, COUNT(*) AS records FROM bus_address WHERE deleted_at IS NULL GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10
